---
import Quickstart from "../../components/landing/Quickstart";
import { consumer, provider } from "../../data/examples";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Quickstart — Agent Interaction Kit"
  description="Build AIK, describe your provider and consumer contracts, and catch incompatible tool changes in CI."
>
  <article class="docs shell">
    <a class="text-link" href="/">← Back to AIK</a><span class="eyebrow">QUICKSTART</span>
    <h1>Make the agreement<br /><em>explicit.</em></h1>
    <p class="docs-lead">
      Two JSON manifests describe the tool contract between your agent and your interface. AIK
      checks whether they agree.
    </p>
    <h2>01 / Build AIK</h2><p>
      Use Node.js 24 and pnpm 10.26.1 for this repository. The npm release is in preparation; build
      the CLI locally today.
    </p>
    <Quickstart client:load />
    <h2>02 / Describe both sides</h2><p>
      Save these files in <code>packages/core/</code>. They use the same fixtures that are checked
      against the real engine during the website build.
    </p>
    <div class="manifest-downloads">
      <a class="button secondary" href="/examples/provider.json" download="aik.provider.json"
        >Download provider ↓</a
      ><a class="button secondary" href="/examples/consumer.json" download="aik.consumer.json"
        >Download consumer ↓</a
      >
    </div>
    <details>
      <summary>aik.provider.json · what the agent provides</summary><pre
        class="code-block"><code>{JSON.stringify(provider, null, 2)}</code></pre>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>aik.consumer.json · what the UI expects</summary><pre
        class="code-block"><code>{JSON.stringify(consumer, null, 2)}</code></pre>
    </details>
    <h2>03 / Check the contract</h2><p>From the repository root, run:</p><pre
      class="code-block"><code>pnpm --filter @agent-interaction-kit/core aik check \
  --provider aik.provider.json \
  --consumer aik.consumer.json --strict</code></pre>
    <p>
      Rename <code>total</code> to <code>amount</code> in the provider's return schema, including its
      required list. Run again: AIK reports <code>AIK-RESULT-002</code> and exits with code 1. Restore
      the field to pass.
    </p>
    <h2>04 / Use it in CI</h2><p>
      After checkout, pnpm setup and dependency installation, build and check your manifests:
    </p><pre
      class="code-block"><code>- run: pnpm build:core
- run: |
    pnpm --filter @agent-interaction-kit/core aik check \
      --provider aik.provider.json \
      --consumer aik.consumer.json --strict</code></pre>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>Result and exit code with --strict</caption><thead
          ><tr><th>Result</th><th>Exit</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead
        ><tbody
          ><tr><td>Pass</td><td>0</td><td>Compatible within the supported schema checks.</td></tr
          ><tr><td>Fail</td><td>1</td><td>A breaking contract change was detected.</td></tr><tr
            ><td>Unknown / error</td><td>2</td><td
              >Unsupported schema, invalid input or missing files.</td
            ></tr
          ></tbody
        >
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Unknown is not a pass.</strong> Without <code>--strict</code>, an unknown report can
      exit 0. Keep strict mode enabled in CI. For example, a return schema with <code>not</code> is inconclusive
      and emits <code>AIK-SCHEMA-001</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      AIK compares the manifests you supply. It does not validate live traffic, automatically repair
      code, or prove that the implementation matches its manifest. Keep contracts reviewed and
      updated alongside code.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/DevJoaoLopes/agent-interaction-kit"
      >Explore the source and full CLI reference ↗</a
    >
  </article>
</Layout>
